<div class="wrap content-grid"><article class="prose">
<p class="lead">Every tool on CPA Calculator is built on one principle: make complex tax and accounting math accurate, understandable and usable by people who aren’t accountants. This page explains how we do that, so you can judge the results for yourself.</p>

<h2 id="sources">1. Primary sources only</h2>
<p>Tax figures come straight from official publications, never from other calculators or secondary blogs:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>IRS revenue procedures</strong> for annual inflation adjustments — Rev. Proc. 2025-32 for <?= $Y ?> brackets, standard deductions and credits; Rev. Proc. 2024-40 for 2025.</li>
  <li><strong>IRS notices and news releases</strong> for the standard mileage rate, retirement plan limits and HSA limits.</li>
  <li><strong>Social Security Administration</strong> announcements for the contribution and benefit base (<?= money($y['ssWageBase']) ?> for <?= $Y ?>).</li>
  <li><strong>The Internal Revenue Code and Treasury regulations</strong>, including the changes made by the One Big Beautiful Bill Act of 2025 (tips, overtime and senior deductions; the <?= money($y['saltCap']) ?> SALT cap; the permanent 20% QBI deduction; 100% bonus depreciation).</li>
  <li><strong>IRS forms and instructions</strong> — Form 1040, Schedule 1, Schedule C, Schedule SE, Form 8995, Form 1040-ES and Publication 946 (MACRS tables).</li>
</ul>
<p>All figures live in a single data file shared by every calculator and every page, so a number can’t be updated in one place and missed in another.</p>

