<h2 id="order">3. The same order of operations as your tax return</h2>
<p>Our tax engine follows Form 1040 line by line rather than using shortcuts like “multiply income by an average rate”:</p>
<ol>
  <li>Total income from wages, net self-employment profit, interest, dividends and capital gains.</li>
  <li>Self-employment tax on Schedule SE (92.35% of profit; Social Security capped at the wage base after counting W-2 wages).</li>
  <li>Adjustments: pre-tax retirement and HSA contributions, the deduction for half of SE tax, self-employed health insurance.</li>
  <li>The larger of the standard or itemized deduction, then the new below-the-line deductions and the QBI deduction.</li>
  <li>Ordinary income through the brackets, with qualified dividends and long-term gains stacked on top at preferential rates.</li>
  <li>Credits, then the net investment income tax and Additional Medicare tax.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="testing">4. How we test</h2>
<p>Before a calculator is published, it is checked against hand calculations and the IRS’s own worked examples across low, middle and high incomes, every filing status and edge cases where formulas tend to break — income exactly at a bracket threshold, self-employment earnings below $400, combined W-2 and 1099 income above the Social Security wage base, and phase-out ranges. Server-rendered examples on our pages are generated by the same math as the interactive tools, so the numbers in the article always match the calculator.</p>

<h2 id="assumptions">5. Assumptions and limitations</h2>
<p>To stay usable, the calculators make clearly stated simplifications:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Federal only, unless a tool says otherwise. State and local income taxes vary too much to model accurately in a general tool.</li>
  <li>The Alternative Minimum Tax, the Earned Income Tax Credit’s full table and some credit phase-outs are not computed.</li>
  <li>The QBI deduction is shown before the wage/property limits and the specified-service phase-out that apply above the income threshold.</li>
  <li>Inputs are treated as annual totals for the selected year unless a tool asks for per-paycheck figures.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout warn"><?= icon('shield') ?><p>CPA Calculator provides educational estimates, not tax, legal or accounting advice. Use the results to plan and to prepare better questions — then confirm with a licensed CPA or enrolled agent before you file or make a financial decision. See our <a href="/disclaimer/">disclaimer</a>.</p></div>

<h2 id="updates">6. How we keep tools current</h2>
<p>We review every calculator when the IRS publishes the next year’s inflation adjustments (usually October or November), when Congress changes the law and at least once a quarter otherwise. Each calculator page shows the tax year it is built for, and most let you switch between the current and prior year.</p>

<h2 id="content">7. How we write our guides</h2>
<p>Our guides are written in plain English first: short sentences, defined terms and worked examples with real numbers. Claims are tied to IRS sources, examples are computed rather than typed by hand, and articles are dated and updated when rules change. We don’t publish invented statistics or testimonials.</p>

<h2 id="privacy">8. Privacy by design</h2>
<p>All calculations run in your browser. The numbers you type into a CPA Calculator tool are not sent to our servers or stored. Share links put your inputs in the URL only when you choose to copy one. Details are in our <a href="/privacy-policy/">privacy policy</a>.</p>

